<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A hole in the page is a sink">
        <p>A <strong>sink</strong> is a hole the render leaves where a value is still in flight, for
        the browser to fill once that value lands. It is how markup can be sent past a value that
        has not arrived — the bytes around it go now, and the document closes when the walk is done.
        The browser's own call fills the hole, and nothing is written into the document to do
        it.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The hole</th><th>What is filled</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'href={x}'}</code>, any attribute</td><td>the attribute or property, on the element itself</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>class:</code>, <code>style:</code>, <code>bind:</code></td><td>the class, the style property, the bound property</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'<title>{name}</title>'}</code></td><td>the element's <code>.textContent</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'<textarea>{v}</textarea>'}</code></td><td>the element's <code>.defaultValue</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>An attribute sink fills <strong>on the element</strong>, not by re-emitting markup around
        it. That matters for the same reason <code>class:</code> and <code>style:</code> matter
        anywhere: one attribute is written and the rest of the element is untouched, so a value
        arriving late cannot disturb an attribute that arrived on time.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="RCDATA fills through the property">
        <p><code>&lt;title&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> hold <strong>RCDATA</strong>
        — text where markup does not parse — so a sink inside one fills through the element's own
        property rather than through markup. Writing markup into a <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> would
        either be shown as characters or close the element early, and neither is what the page
        meant.</p>

        <p>That is why <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> fills <code>defaultValue</code> rather than
        <code>value</code>: it is the <em>initial</em> content the markup was describing, and a
        reader who has already typed into it has not lost what they typed.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/set-the-title-and-social-preview')}>Head &amp; metadata</a>
        · <a href={url('/templates/bind-a-form-to-state')}>Form binding</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The batching unit is the turn">
        <p>A sink fill batches by <strong>turn</strong>, not by production. A value that produces
        ten times in one turn is one write, not ten — so a stream arriving faster than the page can
        paint does not turn into ten times the work.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Nothing holds the flush but one turn">
        <p>Not a hole, not the head, not the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>. The document is a stream,
        and a value that has not arrived is a hole in it. There is no form that turns one back into
        a wait — which is what makes "the page goes out first" a property rather than a default you
        keep choosing.</p>

        <p>The one wait is abide's, and it is a single turn. The page and its layouts are set up,
        the loads they started get that turn to answer, and then the markup is built. A value
        already held, or a handler with nothing to wait on, is painted rather than left as a hole.
        Anything that waits on the network or a timer is not waited for.</p>

        <p>Nothing has gone out during that turn, so what the page read there still decides the
        answer. A handler that refused — <code>notFound()</code> for a record that is not there — is
        painted by the nearest <code>error.abide</code> at its own status, a 404 rather than a 200
        that stops short (41.1).</p>

        <p>The case that wants otherwise is metadata a crawler reads, which does not come back for
        the second version. That value has to be there before the render starts, and getting it
        there is the server's problem — not a spelling in the template.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/start/how-a-page-becomes-html')}>Request to paint</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/start/how-a-page-becomes-html')}>Request to paint</a> — the whole
            sequence this is one stage of</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/render-a-document-yourself')}>Manual rendering</a> — producing
            the same stream yourself</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/set-the-title-and-social-preview')}>Head &amp; metadata</a> —
            the one place holding earns its cost</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
